{
  "topic": "Complete C/C++ Operator Precedence and Associativity Reference Table",
  "explanation": "Operator precedence determines the order in which operators are evaluated in expressions without explicit parentheses. Associativity determines the direction of evaluation when operators of the same precedence appear consecutively. Understanding this table is essential for correctly interpreting complex expressions and avoiding subtle bugs. Common pitfalls include: confusing bitwise operators (&, |, ^) with logical operators (&&, ||), mixing shift operators with arithmetic, and relying on precedence instead of using explicit parentheses. Best practice is to use parentheses liberally to make the evaluation order explicit, which improves code readability and maintainability even when precedence rules are well understood. This comprehensive reference covers all C and C++ operators organized by precedence level from highest (1) to lowest (17).",
  "syntax": "Common Precedence Pitfalls:\n\n1. Bitwise vs Comparison:\n   if (flags & MASK == 0)    // WRONG: == has higher precedence\n   if ((flags & MASK) == 0)  // CORRECT\n\n2. Shift vs Arithmetic:\n   x << 2 + 1      // Evaluates as: x << (2+1), shifts by 3\n   (x << 2) + 1    // Shifts first, then adds\n\n3. Member Access vs Dereference:\n   *ptr.member     // WRONG if ptr is a pointer\n   (*ptr).member   // CORRECT\n   ptr->member     // PREFERRED\n\n4. Postfix vs Prefix:\n   *p++            // Dereferences p, THEN increments p\n   *++p            // Increments p, THEN dereferences\n   (*p)++          // Increments value pointed to by p\n\n5. Assignment in Conditions:\n   if (x = 5)      // Assignment (always true)\n   if (x == 5)     // Comparison",
  "code_example": "/*\n * C/C++ OPERATOR PRECEDENCE AND ASSOCIATIVITY TABLE\n * Precedence: 1 = Highest, 17 = Lowest\n * Associativity: L->R (Left to Right), R->L (Right to Left)\n */\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 1 - Scope Resolution (Highest Precedence)\n// ========================================================================\n// Operator         Associativity    Description\n// --------         -------------    -----------\n::                  L->R              Scope resolution (C++ only)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 2 - Postfix Operators\n// ========================================================================\na++, a--            L->R              Postfix increment/decrement\ntype()              L->R              Functional cast (C++)\ntype{}              L->R              Functional cast (C++11)\na()                 L->R              Function call\na[]                 L->R              Array subscript\n.                   L->R              Member access\n->                  L->R              Member access through pointer\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 3 - Prefix/Unary Operators\n// ========================================================================\n++a, --a            R->L              Prefix increment/decrement\n+a, -a              R->L              Unary plus/minus\n!, ~                R->L              Logical NOT, bitwise NOT\n(type)              R->L              C-style cast\n*a                  R->L              Dereference (indirection)\n&a                  R->L              Address-of\nsizeof              R->L              Size-of operator\nnew, new[]          R->L              Dynamic allocation (C++)\ndelete, delete[]    R->L              Dynamic deallocation (C++)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 4 - Pointer-to-Member (C++ only)\n// ========================================================================\n.*                  L->R              Pointer-to-member\n->*                 L->R              Pointer-to-member through pointer\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 5 - Multiplicative\n// ========================================================================\na*b, a/b, a%b       L->R              Multiplication, division, modulo\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 6 - Additive\n// ========================================================================\na+b, a-b            L->R              Addition, subtraction\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 7 - Shift\n// ========================================================================\n<<, >>              L->R              Bitwise left shift, right shift\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 8 - Three-Way Comparison (C++20)\n// ========================================================================\n<=>                 L->R              Three-way comparison (spaceship)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 9 - Relational\n// ========================================================================\n<, <=, >, >=        L->R              Relational comparison operators\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 10 - Equality\n// ========================================================================\n==, !=              L->R              Equality operators\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 11 - Bitwise AND\n// ========================================================================\n&                   L->R              Bitwise AND\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 12 - Bitwise XOR\n// ========================================================================\n^                   L->R              Bitwise XOR (exclusive OR)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 13 - Bitwise OR\n// ========================================================================\n|                   L->R              Bitwise OR (inclusive OR)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 14 - Logical AND\n// ========================================================================\n&&                  L->R              Logical AND (short-circuit)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 15 - Logical OR\n// ========================================================================\n||                  L->R              Logical OR (short-circuit)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 16 - Conditional and Assignment\n// ========================================================================\na?b:c               R->L              Ternary conditional operator\nthrow               R->L              Throw exception (C++)\nco_await            R->L              Coroutine await (C++20)\n=                   R->L              Direct assignment\n+=, -=              R->L              Compound assignment (add, subtract)\n*=, /=, %=          R->L              Compound assignment (mult, div, mod)\n<<=, >>=            R->L              Compound assignment (shift)\n&=, ^=, |=          R->L              Compound assignment (bitwise)\n\n// ========================================================================\n// LEVEL 17 - Comma (Lowest Precedence)\n// ========================================================================\n,                   L->R              Comma operator (sequence point)",
  "example_explanation": "This table provides a complete reference for all C and C++ operators organized by precedence level. Level 1 has the highest precedence and Level 17 the lowest. Associativity indicates evaluation direction: Left-to-Right (L->R) means operators are evaluated from left to right (e.g., a-b-c evaluates as (a-b)-c), while Right-to-Left (R->L) means evaluation proceeds from right to left (e.g., a=b=c evaluates as a=(b=c)). Key observations: postfix operators bind tighter than prefix, member access binds tighter than dereference (hence -> convenience), bitwise operators have lower precedence than comparison (a common source of bugs), and assignment has very low precedence. Always use parentheses when mixing operators from different precedence levels to ensure clarity and correctness.",
